{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1159803-problem-coming-up-with-a-campaign-idea-for-my-players","id":1159803,"title":"Problem coming up with a campaign idea for my players","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-11-17T06:01:37-08:00","id":1159803,"title":"Problem coming up with a campaign idea for my players","content":"Hi there I hope you ala wonderful minds with a problem that has come up.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>The setup</b></span><br /> I am a new game master and my players are new to paper rpgs, as I run the first story ,which involves their initiation rite, I started having some ideas and developing them for the overarching following story.<br /> <br /> The plot goes something like this: A ancient human that has been using/worshiping the wyrm for long long time. He thinks any form of society or group bonding weakens humankind and he has been exploiting and toppling kingdoms and empires for millenia and now is ready to strike again. He has several plots, seemingly unrelated that he thinks will make a domino effect that will crash most of the western world.<br /> <br /> I love it, I am constantly thinking new plots and quirks to his character and it would allowed me to run several types of adventures with different flavours but that are tied together.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>The problem</b></span><br /> As the last sessions of the initial story have come to an end I have realized that this won't probably work with my group.<br /> <br /> First comes the players, even with all of them being my friends, I am having trouble reading what they want out of the game and being blunt and asking them has always resulted in some variation of &quot;We don't know, we are new to this, whatever you send us man&quot;.<br /> <br /> Also, for the campaign above to work well there should be a fair amount of investigating and curiosity to find out what is happening in the city, so far they are having trouble with this, they are struggling with what they are capable of in this world outside of: sidestepping, running, sneaking and attacking. And, probably because of some bad balanced encounters on my part a bit afraid of going too far from the &quot;do the minimum and don't be nosy&quot; road.<br /> <br /> To add an example, in a recent game they acquire a mobile phone from a bad guy, but the very next step they decided was to leave to the umbra when then they realized they had lost the phone to the material world (thats how my umbra sidestepping works), They were sad but have resign themselves to it. I stopped the game for a sec and out of character I told the players that they could do anything they wanted in the game and asked the specific player with the phone what would she do if she were in that position, the player said that look into the phone before leaving it and then so they did.<br /> <br /> Second comes the characters, as I would have some trouble tying that particular plot to their characters motivations, partly because they are a bit weird and partly because I suppose I am not that good.<br /> <br /> I have 2 lupus, 1 peasant foreign Metis and 1 homid. Not the best lineup for a investigate in the city adventure.<br /> <br /> One of the lupus is a violent Shadow lord Ragabash with almost 0 backstory and 0 ambition past being alive, he keeps getting the pack in danger (this is not inherently bad) and was a bit of a loner and is learning how to trust and work with a pack (this is great). He has destiny background and hunted trait so I have come up with some prophecy involving a vampire that will crash with him, at some point.<br /> <br /> The other lupus is a Black fury Ahroun that wants to protect her sisters. Hates pimps and human trafficking. Doesn't know if she wants to take the alpha mantle. I am thinking of ways to flesh out more the character.<br /> <br /> The metis comes from a reclusive Stargazers caern in Thailand and has leave his clan because the only Garou who loved him and truly believed in him was killed, he want to see what the wold has to offer and looking for a place to call home. He is the calm, nothing disturbs me, violence is not always the answer archetype but lately has come to appreciate the practicality of firearms.<br /> <br /> The homid is a Fianna that classes with the stereotypical party hard clich\u00e9 of her tribe and thus of her family she has cross the sea to show her family (and herself) that she is strong and as Fianna as any other, just in her own way. This is the character that fits the most in to the plot. <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>The solution</b></span><br /> I am an adult and have no problem ditching the campaign concept, it is my notes and not going anywhere, I can use it in another time. I want a story that my players (and their characters) can feel a connection with. The problem is that I am having trouble coming up with ideas for this pack, almost everything that comes to mind is subconsciously tied to that first idea, it doesn't want to leave my mind.<br /> <br /> I guess that rural and umbral adventures are the best with this pack, 2 players actually had some predisposition to the horror theme so maybe some Cthulhu-esque cult or a nightmare Quimera quest. but nothing solid comes to mind.<br /> <br /> So, can you help me? Anything is appreciated, general tips and ideas, books or adventures to read that can steer me in a good direction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/20067-hares666","name":"Hares666","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar20067_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"14"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-17T06:41:06-08:00","id":1159814,"title":"","content":"I dig the idea. The concept is a neat idea and you should keep with it. But begin a bit smaller. The Black Fury &quot;just so happens&quot; to find a pimp who is using his ladies to poison the clients during sex. The clients don't die right away but end up spreading the poison to their partners and what not. Based on what you were saying, this is a good start to motivate at least one player in character and if they are just going with the flow, you have a nice start to introduce the main villain. The pimp is working for the villain where he obtained the drugs. Now the pack has an enemy they can focus on. They do a little research and find this guy keeps popping up and you should be able to get them into it from there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/19321-kewlimp","name":"kewlimp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"36"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-17T16:05:52-08:00","id":1160033,"title":"","content":"If they have become a bit gun shy as it were scale back the supernatural opposition aspect of the game. A ton of the &quot;wyrms minions&quot; are just rather callous or misguided and easily corrupted regular people. When. Had a group new to Werewolf not so long ago their early investigations led then to a gang (plus a couple of banes n the umbra urging / feeding on their activities should the pack step sideways. No Fomori, no significant swarm of Scags, just gang of humans. Sure they had a couple of 9mm semi automatic handguns, knives and a broken bottle. Sure the pack were not in a situation where they cold go Crinos but they learned just how tough Glabro can be and how fragile humans are. <br /> <br /> A little later on they encountered some Fomori and some Scrags and learned while these things can hurt a Crinos Garou they also tend to get shredded in equal measure by them and the Garou are healed within a minute of all but the worste wounds. They loved it. They lapped it up. They had the terrifying might of being a werewolf. Then they met Fomori in groups, packs of Scrags and other banes or individual more powerful ones. Got shot by silver, ran afoul of BSDs and werewolves from less tan friendly septs or tribes. They were already invested a this point but learned that they cold be seriously put in danger, they started trying to pick their fights as well as deal with problems.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2767-moirdryd","name":"Moirdryd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-17T16:26:19-08:00","id":1160041,"title":"","content":"To build an investigation setup, you have to be carefully seeding clues, even if the characters and the players are not ready to decipher them, yet. Little steps, like a peculiar grafitti on the wall, just where some &quot;random&quot; thugs did hang out. Later, as it turns out, they remember having seen the secret code or identification symbol of the antagonists minions everywhere in the city.<br /> <br /> Also, play a bit with the characters concepts. Two Lupus who don't know much about the city? A Fianna, who doesn't like to party and a calm Stargazer? Sure, no simple party, but what if they just had to pick up a clue about a gang trafficking whores or whatever in a very dirty but well visited club? When the Fury gets hyped, she will need the others. Maybe the Shadowlord will understand just how much they need each other and whatnot. But it wouldn't be an easy fight. There are lots of people around and clues are hard to find, so take a look at all their abilities and design some challanges for all of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13221-thevarulfen","name":"TheVarulfen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"179"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-17T21:06:22-08:00","id":1160111,"title":"","content":"I would toss a few fomorii at them, let them know there are plenty of Wyrm minions out there that even low-ranking Garou can handle.<br /> <br /> I would also make sure there are friendly and sympathetic NPC Garou, so you can establish you as a GM are trying to populate a world filled with multifaceted characters, rather than piles of enemies and Elders looking to bust the pack's balls. Garou are confrontational creatures, but unrelenting and constant conflict can make the game seem weirdly adversarial, and many folks don't really seem to enjoy that.<br /> <br /> It sounds like you knew your 'foe calibration' was off, so with that in mind, I would take pains to reward whatever cautious world-exploration they engage in. Garou are dangerous creatures, but early on players and characters both need to just take some time learning about the world and poking things. Maybe they can find a lost fetish in the most surprising of places, or an injured spirit they can save and befriend. Perhaps they just find something really weird, like a wacky Gorgon or escaped Samsa, getting a glimpse of something that can either be chased down or encountered later (or not, if they seem bored or uninterested).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3695-the-laughing-stranger","name":"The Laughing Stranger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3695_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1010"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-18T11:15:24-08:00","id":1160253,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moirdryd</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1159803-problem-coming-up-with-a-campaign-idea-for-my-players?p=1160033#post1160033\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> No Fomori, no significant swarm of Scags, just gang of humans. Sure they had a couple of 9mm semi automatic handguns, knives and a broken bottle. Sure the pack were not in a situation where they cold go Crinos but they learned just how tough Glabro can be and how fragile humans are.<br /> <br /> A little later on they encountered some Fomori and some Scrags and learned while these things can hurt a Crinos Garou they also tend to get shredded in equal measure by them and the Garou are healed within a minute of all but the worste wounds.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like this, I think I have sent them a bit too big foes and they love hispo but haven't touch the glabro form so this could work.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kewlimp</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1159803-problem-coming-up-with-a-campaign-idea-for-my-players?p=1159814#post1159814\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I dig the idea. The concept is a neat idea and you should keep with it. But begin a bit smaller. The Black Fury &quot;just so happens&quot; to find a pimp who is using his ladies to poison the clients during sex. The clients don't die right away but end up spreading the poison to their partners and what not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, the Black Fury is the easier to hook but the posion spreading to clients and &quot;good families&quot; is a neat idea, thanks!<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/20067-hares666","name":"Hares666","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar20067_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"14"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-18T11:35:33-08:00","id":1160258,"title":"","content":"Before introducing the main plot, you may want to take the next few game sessions running various one shot plots that introduce the kind of adventures available to the Garou. That way your PCs see what is available to them, and you can see what they like and dislike. This is especially easy to do with Cliath packs as sept leaders can order them to do various things. And then once you know what your PCs like, when they are Fostern or almost ready to be Fostern, then you can move onto your main plot, whatever they may be. So keep one or more minor antagonists which can be totally defeated.<br /> <br /> 1) Have some industrial waste site that is low priority, but is known to produce various fomori (these can be animal fomori if you want, like rats, vermin, birds or stray animals who wander the site). Every now and again, the Sept Leader sends a young Cliath pack out to roust and slay whatever is there. This would be a tactical combat exercise, and you'd be describing the waste site, allowing the fomori lots of places to hide, and the PCs need to make various tactical decisions on scouting, exploring, learning how to deal with ambushes, etc. The individual fomori would be fairly weak, but when together can be formidable. So you can have the party do a few easy encounters, and then present them with a big mass of them to see how they'll respond. You can also include some minor banes in the Umbra to serve as foes if they cross over there.<br /> <br /> 2) Present them a minor mystery. For example, while exploring the industrial waste site, they come across some paperwork on a clipboard indicating some of the industrial waste is being moved. They only see that it's been picked up and a destination address. If they investigate, they find out the address is to a green site - an old abandoned lumber mill closed down since the early twentieth century. But the land has recently been bought and been excavated in order to store this waste. Then you can run a series of investigations if they want to figure out why is this waste being moved, who bought this land, why are they allowing this company to move it, etc.<br /> <br /> 3) Present them with a threat among the Garou and issues involving the Litany. This is an urban environment so maybe you can have a small pack of Bone Gnawers who have learned human flesh is delicious and begun to prey upon people in the city who won't be missed - long term homeless who are mentally ill, drug addicts, and street prostitutes. Does the pack attack them? Do they inform the sept's leadership? Do they try to collect evidence beforehand? Does the man eating pack notice them? How does this play out in sept politics? Do the other Bone Gnawers not really care? Are there hard core Litany supporters who demand execution, while others say it should be treated like a drug addiction and them placed under supervision to help them? You can introduce a lot of conflict between how different tribes interpret things. What if the man eaters turn it around and begin accusations of the PCs of not respecting their territory, or that the PCs simply misunderstood what happened when they were attacking fomori in Hispo using bite attacks? Depending on how the PCs pursue this, the end result can be very different.<br /> <br /> 4) Do some typical Werewolf cinematic and literary plots. Run a plot with an NPC going through her First Change in the city, and the pack is the first to encounter her. How can they end the Frenzy, protect the Veil, and transport the NPC safely to the sept. Or perhaps there is a serial killer in the city who turns out to be a Lunatic, a Garou who has been driven mad by never Changing. This can be a gruesome killer, but it is because they're dealing with an Ahroun's Rage for years while never transforming. So the pack can come across the killer, but somehow figure out the person is actually a Garou. How do they react? Introduce them to a Ronin who rejects the Garou ways as some kind of satanic cult and who seeks to &quot;save&quot; other newly changed Garou from this cult (the sept) and thus come into conflict with the PCs. This Ronin has tried various ways to &quot;cure&quot; Garou, which come down to various drug remedies which end up reducing Rage and Gnosis which reduces the emotional pain they feel (Rage) and their spiritual connection to Gaia (Gnosis) so it makes people feel like an ordinary human once more (in both good and bad ways).<br /> <br /> 5) If you think your PCs would be interested in some of the social problem aspects, then you can introduce a minor Jaggling of one of the Urge Wyrms in the umbra who is encouraging whatever specific problem you want to showcase - drug addiction, spousal or child abuse, neglect, suicide, etc. Have the PC most likely to be interested come across this problem to engage in it, and then they can see subtle clues that it is being spiritually manipulated as well. Then the pack can come in and deal with the Banes in the Umbra making the problem worse.<br /> <br /> 6) Run a minor plot that is straight out Horror. Someone in a seedy head shop has come across a minor magical tome that actually has connections to a minor Wyrm entity, like a Jaggling servant of whatever BSD totem you find most interesting. The person and his friends attempt to summon it, and it happens, and a new Wyrm cult forms. They begin to go out and do whatever evil the totem requires in exchange for benefits. The PCs realize something supernatural is going on, they investigate it, track the cultists down to their lair, and then they fight a materialized Jaggling that looks however you'd like and have whatever powers you need.<br /> <br /> If you ever need to give advice or point PCs in the right direction, you can use their Pack Totem as a means to do so. Roleplay its personality and be honest to what the spirit wants.<br /> <br /> By doing this the PCs will get an idea of the flavors of the game, and at that time you can have the right conversation about what they like and don't like. And in the meantime, they'll have a wide variety of adventures and different Renown opportunities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}